@module dart-m01 | Introduction to Dart
@summary Meet Dart, set up your tools and write, run and understand your first programs.

@lesson dart-m01-l01 | What is Dart? | 10
## Learning objectives
- Explain what Dart is and who maintains it
- Describe how Dart compiles for development and for production
- Recognise why Flutter uses Dart

## A language built for apps
Dart is an open-source, general-purpose programming language created at Google and first released in 2011. Today it is best known as the language behind **Flutter**, Google's UI toolkit for building apps for mobile, web, desktop and embedded devices from one codebase. Dart is also used on its own for command-line tools, servers and scripts.

Dart is a **statically typed**, **object-oriented** language with a familiar C-style syntax. If you have seen Java, C#, Kotlin, Swift or JavaScript, Dart code will look recognisable within minutes. Every value in Dart is an object, even numbers and functions, and every object is an instance of a class.

## Two ways to compile
Dart is unusual because it has two compilation strategies, and Flutter uses both:

- **JIT (just-in-time)** compilation during development. The Dart VM compiles code as it runs, which enables Flutter's famous *hot reload*: you change code and see the result in under a second without losing app state.
- **AOT (ahead-of-time)** compilation for release. Your code is compiled to fast native ARM or x64 machine code, so apps start quickly and run smoothly.

Dart can also compile to **JavaScript** and to **WebAssembly** for the web.

```dart
void main() {
  // Every Dart program starts at main().
  var language = 'Dart';
  print('Hello from $language!');
}
```

This tiny program declares a variable and prints a message using *string interpolation* (`$language`). You will learn each piece in the next lessons.

## Sound null safety
Since Dart 3, the language is **100% sound null safe**. A variable of type `String` can never contain `null`; if you want to allow `null` you must write `String?`. The compiler checks this for you, which eliminates a whole class of crashes before your app ever runs.

> NOTE: "Sound" means the type system gives a guarantee, not a hint. If the compiler says a value is non-nullable, it really is non-nullable at runtime.

## Why Flutter chose Dart
- Fast development cycles thanks to JIT and hot reload
- Fast, predictable production performance thanks to AOT compilation
- A garbage collector tuned for creating many short-lived objects, which is exactly what UI frameworks do
- One language for UI and logic: no separate markup language

:::check Which compilation mode makes hot reload possible?
JIT (just-in-time) compilation in the Dart VM during development.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Thinking Dart is "just for Flutter". It is a complete language with its own server, CLI and web ecosystem.
- Confusing Dart with JavaScript. Dart can compile *to* JavaScript, but it is a separate, statically typed language.

## Summary
- Dart is an open-source, statically typed, object-oriented language from Google.
- It uses JIT for fast development and AOT for fast production code.
- Dart 3 is fully sound null safe.
- Flutter uses Dart for hot reload, performance and a single-language workflow.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Overview
- dart.dev ▸ Language tour
@quiz
? mcq: Which company created and maintains the Dart language?
- Microsoft
+ Google
- Apple
- JetBrains
> Dart was created at Google and is developed as an open-source project.
? mcq: Which compilation strategy produces fast native code for release builds?
- JIT compilation
+ AOT compilation
- Interpretation only
- Transpiling to Java bytecode
> Ahead-of-time (AOT) compilation turns Dart into native machine code for production.
? tf: In Dart 3, a variable of type `String` can hold `null`.
= false
> Dart 3 is fully sound null safe; only `String?` can hold null.
? scenario: A teammate asks why Flutter developers can see UI changes in under a second while coding. What is the best answer?
- Dart is interpreted line by line in production
+ The Dart VM's JIT compiler supports hot reload during development
- Flutter re-downloads the app from the store
- Android Studio caches screenshots
> Hot reload relies on JIT compilation in the Dart VM, which injects updated code into the running app.
? fill: Dart can compile to native code, JavaScript and Web___.
= Assembly | assembly
> Dart can also target WebAssembly (dart2wasm) for the web.
? mcq: Which statement about Dart values is true?
- Only class instances are objects; numbers are primitives
+ Every value is an object, including numbers and functions
- Functions cannot be stored in variables
- Dart has no classes
> In Dart everything you can place in a variable is an object.

@lesson dart-m01-l02 | The Dart ecosystem | 10
## Learning objectives
- Identify the main tools that ship with the Dart SDK
- Understand what pub and pub.dev are
- Know where Dart runs: mobile, web, desktop, server and CLI

## More than a compiler
When you install Dart (or Flutter, which bundles Dart) you get a complete toolkit driven by a single command: `dart`. Knowing these tools early saves hours later.

```bash
dart create my_app        # create a new project from a template
dart run                  # run the project
dart format .             # format code in the official style
dart analyze              # static analysis: errors, warnings, lints
dart test                 # run tests (with package:test)
dart fix --apply          # automatically apply suggested fixes
dart compile exe bin/my_app.dart   # build a native executable
dart pub get              # download dependencies
```

Each command does one job well. `dart format` ends style debates because everyone uses the same formatter. `dart analyze` catches bugs before you run anything, and `dart fix` can migrate code when APIs change.

## Packages, pub and pub.dev
Dart's package manager is called **pub**. Your project lists its dependencies in a file called `pubspec.yaml`, and `dart pub get` downloads them. Public packages are published on **pub.dev**, which hosts tens of thousands of packages such as `http`, `path`, `intl` and `collection`.

```yaml
name: my_app
environment:
  sdk: ^3.5.0
dependencies:
  http: ^1.2.0
dev_dependencies:
  test: ^1.25.0
  lints: ^4.0.0
```

The `^` (caret) means "compatible with": `^1.2.0` accepts any `1.x.y` version from 1.2.0 upwards but not 2.0.0.

## Core libraries
Dart comes with core libraries you can import without adding dependencies:
- `dart:core` (imported automatically): `String`, `int`, `List`, `Map`, `print` and more
- `dart:async`: `Future`, `Stream`, timers
- `dart:math`: random numbers, `max`, `min`, `pi`
- `dart:convert`: JSON and UTF-8 encoding
- `dart:io`: files, sockets, HTTP servers (not available on the web)

## Where Dart runs
- **Mobile, desktop and embedded** through Flutter, compiled AOT to native code
- **Web** through Flutter web or plain Dart compiled to JavaScript/WebAssembly
- **Servers and CLIs** through the Dart VM or native executables

> TIP: Run `dart --help` and `dart <command> --help` whenever you forget an option. The built-in help is excellent.

:::check What file lists a Dart project's dependencies?
`pubspec.yaml`
:::

## Common mistakes
- Editing the generated `pubspec.lock` by hand. Let pub manage it.
- Using `dart:io` in code meant for the web; it is not supported there.

## Summary
- The `dart` command bundles create, run, format, analyze, test, fix and compile.
- pub manages dependencies declared in `pubspec.yaml`; packages live on pub.dev.
- Core libraries like `dart:async`, `dart:math` and `dart:convert` need no installation.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Tools ▸ dart command-line tool
- pub.dev ▸ Most popular packages
@quiz
